---
// Marks a `position:sticky` bar `.is-stuck` from the moment it pins under the fixed site header.
// That class is what fades in the frosted-glass backdrop behind the store's category chips
// (`.store-controls::before`, styles/pages/store.css), what cancels the header's own shadow on the
// homepage tab strip, and what tells the store banner's curtain it is covering something.
//
// **Why this is an `is:inline` script and not a call inside the page's module** (2026-08-18, owner:
// *"יש פעמים שהפס גלאס מורפיזם שמאחורי הקטגוריות … לא נטען, ואז אין שם כלום"*).
//
// It used to be `initStickyBar()` in `lib/sticky-bar.ts`, called from the bottom of each page's own
// `<script>`. A page module is deferred AND is the tail of the page's whole import graph — the
// store page pulls in a dozen modules — so `.is-stuck` could only ever land after every one of them
// had downloaded and run. The bar itself is CSS: it pins on the first pixel of scroll, glass or no
// glass. Measured on the store page at 400kbps / 6x CPU throttle: the bar was pinned at ~4.4s and
// still had no `.is-stuck` at 10s. On a cold cache, a slow phone, or any page-module error at all,
// the shopper scrolls a pinned bar with a fully transparent background over moving product cards —
// exactly "there is nothing there", and exactly "sometimes", because it depends on the network.
//
// Inline, this runs at parse time with nothing to download, so the glass is armed before the first
// scroll can happen. Participation is declared in the markup — `data-sticky-glass` on the bar, its
// value the header offset in rem — so this file stays the one definition and no page repeats the
// logic. `window.__armStickyGlass()` re-arms after a DOM swap (the /stores directory replaces the
// whole results block, sentinel included, on every category change).
//
// BaseLayout renders it BEFORE `<main>`, so it executes almost immediately — and it then watches
// the parse rather than waiting for it, so a bar is dressed the frame it appears. See the
// MutationObserver below.
//
// Plain ES5-ish JS with `var`, like FormFallbackGuard.astro's inline boot, for the same reason:
// `is:inline` is shipped verbatim, so it is never compiled and never bundled.
---

<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    /** Default header offset, in rem — matches `body { padding-top }` and the bars' own `top`. */
    var DEFAULT_REM = 3.3;

    function arm(bar) {
      if (!bar || !bar.parentElement || bar.hasAttribute('data-sticky-glass-armed')) return;
      bar.setAttribute('data-sticky-glass-armed', '');
      // A zero-height sentinel just above the bar: it leaves the viewport at the exact scroll
      // position the bar reaches its own `top`, so one IntersectionObserver replaces a scroll
      // handler and there is no per-frame layout cost.
      var sentinel = document.createElement('div');
      sentinel.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      bar.parentElement.insertBefore(sentinel, bar);
      var rem = parseFloat(bar.getAttribute('data-sticky-glass')) || DEFAULT_REM;
      var rootPx = parseFloat(getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).fontSize) || 16;
      new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
        bar.classList.toggle('is-stuck', !(entries[0] && entries[0].isIntersecting));
      }, { rootMargin: '-' + Math.round(rem * rootPx) + 'px 0px 0px 0px', threshold: 0 }).observe(sentinel);
    }

    function armAll() {
      var bars = document.querySelectorAll('[data-sticky-glass]');
      for (var i = 0; i < bars.length; i++) arm(bars[i]);
    }

    window.__armStickyGlass = armAll;
    armAll();

    // **Armed DURING the parse, not after it.** BaseLayout renders this right after the header, so
    // the bars a page declares further down do not exist yet on that first call — and waiting for
    // DOMContentLoaded would mean waiting for the whole page's HTML, which on a slow connection is
    // seconds of scrollable content. Measured on the store page at 400kbps / 6x CPU: the bar was
    // pinned 1.75s before DOMContentLoaded fired. So watch the document fill instead, and arm each
    // bar the moment the parser has produced it.
    //
    // Coalesced through rAF rather than run per mutation: during a parse the callback fires for
    // every node, and `armAll` is a document-wide querySelectorAll. Per frame it is a handful of
    // cheap scans; per node it would be the kind of quadratic work that hurts most on exactly the
    // slow device this exists to serve. Disconnected at DOMContentLoaded — after that the only
    // source of new bars is a deliberate DOM swap, which calls `__armStickyGlass` itself.
    if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
      var queued = false;
      var mo = new MutationObserver(function () {
        if (queued) return;
        queued = true;
        requestAnimationFrame(function () { queued = false; armAll(); });
      });
      mo.observe(document.documentElement, { childList: true, subtree: true });
      document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () { mo.disconnect(); armAll(); });
    }
  })();
</script>
